# SYNOPSIS

```swift
public func lower(spirv: [UInt32], stage: ShaderStage, entryPoint: String,
                  layout: LoweringLayout,
                  specialization: [SpecConstant] = [])
  throws(LoweringError) -> [UInt32]

public struct SpecConstant { public var id: UInt32, value: UInt64 }

public struct LoweringLayout {
  public func dynamicBase(set: Int) -> Int
  public var sets: [SetLayout?]
  public var pushConstantBytes: Int
  public var vertexBindings: [VertexBinding]
  public var vertexAttributes: [VertexAttribute]
}
public struct SetLayout {
  public init(bindings: [DescriptorBinding])
  public func find(_ binding: UInt32)
    -> (offset: UInt32, binding: DescriptorBinding)?
  public func dynamicIndex(_ binding: UInt32) -> Int?
}
public struct DescriptorBinding {
  public var binding: UInt32, count: UInt32
  public var kind: DescriptorKind
  public var dynamic: Bool
}
public enum DescriptorKind {
  case uniformBuffer, storageBuffer, sampledImage, sampler
  case combinedImageSampler, storageImage
  case uniformTexelBuffer, storageTexelBuffer, inputAttachment
}
public enum RootBlock { /* the root block's offsets and sizes */ }
public enum VertexFormat { /* r32Float ... rgba32Sint, rgba8Unorm */ }
public enum ShaderStage {
  case vertex, fragment, compute
  case geometry, tessellationControl, tessellationEvaluation
}
public enum LoweringError: Error {
  case unsupported(String), invalid(String), malformed(String)
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A Vulkan program names its resources by descriptor set and binding, its constants by a push-constant block and, in a vertex shader, its inputs by location.
The core (gpucore(2)) has one pipeline layout: the bindless heap, and a push-constant block of one buffer reference, the root.
`lower` rewrites the SPIR-V of one stage of one pipeline so that the core's pipelines take it unchanged on every GPU.
It is pure: the same words and layout always give the same words, and it is the same for every backend.
It takes the words of the module, the stage and the entry point's name, and the pipeline layout the program was made against; it gives the module's words rewritten.
What the program doesn't use of these, and everything the rewrite doesn't change (its other functions, its outputs and the interfaces between stages, its decorations, names and execution modes), stays as it was.
A program with nothing to lower comes out as it went in.

Specialization constants.
`specialization` gives the value of each constant by its `SpecId`: the low bytes of the little-endian bytes of its map entry, and a boolean is any non-zero value.
Every `OpSpecConstant`, `OpSpecConstantTrue` and `OpSpecConstantFalse` takes its value (the default, where none is given) and becomes a plain constant; `OpSpecConstantComposite` becomes `OpConstantComposite`; and `OpSpecConstantOp` is evaluated and becomes a constant, or a constant composite with a constant for each element.
The operations are the ones the SPIR-V specification lets `OpSpecConstantOp` name for shaders: `UConvert`, `SConvert`, `FConvert` (between 16, 32 and 64 bits, rounding to nearest even), `SNegate`, `Not`, the integer and bitwise arithmetic and shifts, the logical operations, the comparisons, `Select`, `VectorShuffle`, `CompositeExtract` and `CompositeInsert`, on scalars and, component by component, on vectors.
A constant that is a `WorkgroupSize` built-in, and a `LocalSizeId` execution mode, become `LocalSize` of their values, so the compiler reads a literal workgroup size.
The `SpecId` decorations are gone; the module has no specialization constants left.
A division by zero, an operation outside that list and a constant out of range are errors (`invalid`, `unsupported`, `malformed`).
Only the entry point named, of the stage given, is kept in the module.

The root block is 288 bytes, which the root pointer points at, built for each draw or dispatch by the layer's driver:

```
offset   0   u64 set[4]            the bound set records' addresses (0: none)
offset  32   u64 vertexBuffer[8]   the vertex buffers' addresses plus offsets
offset  96   the push constants, up to 128 bytes
offset 224   u32 dynamicOffset[16]  the bound sets' dynamic offsets
```

A set record holds each binding's descriptors in binding order, each `descriptorSize` (16) bytes, binding `b`'s element `i` at the offset `SetLayout` gives `b` plus 16 times `i`:

```
uniform or storage buffer    u64 address, u64 range
sampled image, sampler,
storage image, input         u32 slot (its heap slot)
attachment, uniform texel
buffer, storage texel buffer
combined image sampler       u32 textureSlot, u32 samplerSlot
```

A heap slot belongs to the object: an image view takes its slot when it is made and a sampler takes its slot when it is made.
The addresses of the root block, the set records and the buffers are multiples of 16, which the code's loads and stores rely on; so are the vertex buffers' addresses, plus their offsets, to 4.

Buffers.
A `Uniform` or `StorageBuffer` variable with a set and a binding (alone or an array of them) is gone, and the pointer types that derive from it are pointers to `PhysicalStorageBuffer` memory.
Each use reads the root pointer, the set record's address and the descriptor's address, and makes a pointer of it with `OpConvertUToPtr`; a function reads each plain binding it uses once, at its start.
Every load and store through such a pointer gets an `Aligned` operand, the natural alignment of what it moves.
The Block and Offset decorations, the std140 and std430 layouts and the `ArrayStride`s stay; a block of the old `BufferBlock` kind becomes a `Block`.
`OpArrayLength` of a buffer is its descriptor's range, less the runtime array's offset, over its stride.
The index into an array of buffers is read at run time, from the descriptor at `offset + 16 * index`.
A binding made dynamic (`DescriptorBinding.dynamic`, for the dynamic uniform and storage buffers) has one more read, of one 4-byte word of the root block, which is added to the descriptor's address before the pointer is made.
The words are numbered as Vulkan numbers the offsets of a bind: the sets in order, within a set the bindings in order, within a binding its elements, so `LoweringLayout.dynamicBase(set:)` plus `SetLayout.dynamicIndex(_:)` plus the element is the word.
The descriptor's range is as written; the offset moves the start, not the length.
A layout with more than 16 dynamic descriptors is `invalid`.

Images and samplers.
A sampled image, a sampler, a combined image sampler, a storage image, a texel buffer or an input attachment variable in `UniformConstant` is gone, and a load of it becomes a load of an element of the heap's array at the slot the set record holds: set 0, binding 0, a runtime array of the program's own image type (`texture1D`, `texture2D`); binding 1, the samplers; binding 2, the storage images; binding 3, the uniform texel buffers (`samplerBuffer`); binding 4, the storage texel buffers (`imageBuffer`).
A combined image sampler is two loads, its texture and its sampler, joined by `OpSampledImage`.
An array of them (indexed once, by a constant or by a value) reads the descriptor `offset + 16 * index`.
A heap access whose element index isn't a constant of the program is decorated `NonUniform`, with the `ShaderNonUniform` and `SampledImageArrayNonUniformIndexing` (or the storage image's) capabilities: the compiler takes a handle that differs between lanes by looping over the distinct ones.
The images are 1D (arrayed or not) or 2D (not arrayed), single-sampled and not depth images, or buffer images, or subpass inputs.
A 1D image is the heap's like a 2D one: the compiler reads and writes its texels (sampled, fetched and storage), and an array of 1D images has a layer.
A storage texel buffer's `OpImageTexelPointer` (an atomic add, say) is the element of the binding 4 array at the slot, named as a pointer, so the atomic works on the texel.
The compiler takes the buffer images (`OpImageFetch` of a `samplerBuffer`, and `OpImageRead`, `OpImageWrite` and `OpImageTexelPointer` of an `imageBuffer` whose format the program says) as the same accesses as a storage image's, with the element index for a coordinate; the Valhall backend refuses them by name (valhallbackend(2)).
An input attachment (`subpassLoad`, an `OpImageRead` of a `SubpassData` image at coordinate (0, 0)) is a fetch: the variable's slot is the texture of the attachment's view, in a `texture2D` of the same texel type, and the read becomes `OpImageFetch` of it at the integer position of the fragment (`FragCoord`'s x and y, truncated, read once at the top of the function; the built-in is declared if the program has none), at level 0.
The `InputAttachment` capability is dropped, since nothing of it is left.
An image operand of `SignExtend` or `ZeroExtend` on a storage image's read or write changes nothing and is dropped.

Push constants.
The program's `PushConstant` variable is gone, and its pointer types, in `PhysicalStorageBuffer`, point at the push constants in the root block (the root pointer plus 96).
The core's own push-constant block, the root, replaces it.
Its one member is a reference to the first part of the root block, which is `{ u64 set[4]; u64 vertexBuffer[8]; }`.
The layout's `pushConstantBytes` may not go over 128.

Vertex input.
In a vertex shader, each `Input` variable with a `Location` (not a built-in) is a `Private` variable that the entry function sets before anything else, from the attribute at its location: `vertexBuffer[binding] + index * stride + offset`, in words, with `index` the `VertexIndex` built-in or, for a per-instance binding, the `InstanceIndex`.
Each is converted by the attribute's format: a 32-bit float, unsigned or signed integer, or `rgba8Unorm` (each byte over 255) to the program's float, `uint` or `int` (or a vector of them, of as many components as the program has).
A component the format doesn't have is 0, and 1 for the fourth, as Vulkan says.
The built-ins are declared if the program doesn't, and are shared with the program's own.
The inputs are no longer in the entry point's interface (those of SPIR-V before 1.4), and are `Private` variables in it where it lists them all.
A float input needs a float attribute and an integer input an integer one.
Offsets and strides are multiples of 4.

The output declares the capabilities `Int64` and `PhysicalStorageBufferAddresses` (and `RuntimeDescriptorArray` for the heap), the extensions they need in a module older than SPIR-V 1.5, and the `PhysicalStorageBuffer64` addressing model.
It is valid SPIR-V, and is taken by the compiler's reader (shadercompiler(2)) as far as the program itself is.

Errors.
`unsupported` names what the layer leaves out, `invalid` what the program and the layout disagree about, `malformed` what isn't SPIR-V.
What it leaves out: an image that isn't 1D, 2D, a buffer or a subpass input (`a 3D image`, `a cube image`, `a rect image`), `an arrayed image` (but a 1D one), `a multisampled image` (and `a multisampled input attachment`), `a depth image`, `an array of arrays of descriptors`, `a pointer access chain into a descriptor`, `a pointer access chain into an array of buffers`, `an access chain into an element of a descriptor array`, a descriptor, an array of buffers or an element of a descriptor array named as a whole (passed to a function, copied, selected between), `OpArrayLength of anything but a buffer`, and a vertex input that is a matrix (`a matrix vertex input`), an array, a structure or not 32 bits wide (`a vertex input of 64-bit type`).
A descriptor the program uses that the layout lacks (its set, its binding, or the kind), a vertex input with no attribute, a push constant block over 128 bytes, a stage that isn't the entry point's, or an entry point that isn't there, are `invalid`.
A binding the program declares and never uses needs nothing of the layout.

